            <ArticleTitle>Exfoliative dermatitis associated with diltiazem.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>101-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Exfoliative dermatitis due to calcium channel blockers is a known but infrequent response.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 77-year-old woman began treatment with diltiazem for angina pectoris. Three days later, severe exfoliative dermatitis developed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The present case and the others previously reported emphasize the need for greater awareness of the occurrence of adverse cutaneous reactions including toxic epidermal necrolysis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome and cutaneous vasculitis due to calcium channel blockers including diltiazem.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
